WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:12.140
<v Ira Glass>A friend in South Africa sent me the link to this article that is making the rounds there. It's an open letter, supposedly from the foreign media, to every man, woman, and child in their country.
<v Ira Glass>我在南非的一位朋友给我发来了这篇文章的链接，这篇文章正在当地广为流传。这是一封公开信，据说是由外国媒体写给该国每一位男人、女人和孩子的。

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:31.710
<v Ira Glass>It begins, "Dear South Africa, please get the F of the way. Wait, that probably came out wrong. As you may have noted, we're back!" Meaning of course, that the foreign press has returned to South Africa to cover what is happening now with Nelson Mandela. I'll go back to the letter.
<v Ira Glass>开头是这样的：“亲爱的南非，请滚开。等等，这话说得可能不对。正如你们可能注意到的，我们回来了！”当然，这意味着外国媒体已经回到南非，报道纳尔逊·曼德拉目前的情况。我回到信的内容。

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:42.000
<v Ira Glass>"In the real world, far away from your sleepy backwater, news works on a 24-hour cycle, and you need to get out of the way. It's nothing personal. In fact, we couldn't do this successfully without you.
<v Ira Glass>“在现实世界中，远离你们那昏昏欲睡的偏远地区，新闻是以24小时为周期运作的，你们需要让开。这没什么个人恩怨。事实上，没有你们，我们无法成功做到这一点。

00:00:42.000 --> 00:01:03.420
<v Ira Glass>In many cases, our footage is made more compelling by your presence. Specifically, we are fond of small black children praying and slash or singing in unison. Equally telegenic are the Aryan ubermensch blond kids, also praying slash singing, who help underscore the theme that Mandela united people of all races under a rainbow umbrella.
<v Ira Glass>在许多情况下，你们的存在使我们的镜头更具感染力。具体来说，我们喜欢那些祈祷和/或齐声歌唱的黑人小孩。同样上镜的还有雅利安超人式的金发小孩，他们也在祈祷/歌唱，这有助于强调曼德拉在彩虹伞下团结了所有种族人民的主题。

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:22.270
<v Ira Glass>Also very important, thematically speaking, are Mandela's successors. We very much like the idea that your ex-president was quote, 'one of a kind', and that despite his best efforts, the current batch of idiots proved that he was an exceptional presence. Sui generis. And we don't have to worry about someone else like him coming along in Africa ever again.
<v Ira Glass>此外，从主题上讲，非常重要的是曼德拉的继任者们。我们非常喜欢这样的观点：你们的前总统是‘独一无二的’，尽管他尽了最大努力，但目前这批白痴证明了他是一个非凡的存在。独一无二。我们再也不用担心非洲会再出现像他这样的人了。

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:35.340
<v Ira Glass>We enjoy your leaders' bumbling ways, their daft nonsequiturs. This story would lack a tragic arc without Jacob Zuma." That of course, is the current president of the country. "May he keep on keeping on."
<v Ira Glass>我们喜欢你们领导人笨拙的方式，他们愚蠢的离题话。没有雅各布·祖马，这个故事将缺少一个悲剧性的弧线。”当然，这就是该国现任总统。“愿他继续保持下去。”

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:51.400
<v Ira Glass>The letter continues. "Then there's the Mandela family. Really, where would we derive our soap operatic undertones if it weren't for the infighting and blinged-up brashness of that clan? We love subtly implying that a saint sired a generation of professional shoppers, no-goodniks.
<v Ira Glass>信中继续写道。“还有曼德拉家族。真的，如果不是因为那个家族的内斗和浮华的炫耀，我们从哪里获得肥皂剧般的底蕴呢？我们喜欢含蓄地暗示，一位圣人竟然生出了这一代只会购物的专业人士和无赖。”

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:59.030
<v Ira Glass>In our biz we call that irony. Makes for great copy. In fact, we love everything about the country that doesn't live up to Mandela's legacy.
<v Ira Glass>在我们的行业里，我们称之为讽刺。这能写出很棒的稿子。事实上，我们喜欢这个国家所有不符合曼德拉遗产的一切。

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:20.460
<v Ira Glass>We will take every opportunity to mention how everything you do flies in the face of everything Mandela would have wanted for his people, how you're basically a nation of underachieving screw ups. All of this is fantastic. We thank you profusely for your individual and collective contributions to this essential storyline, and urge you to keep squandering your potential."
<v Ira Glass>我们将抓住每一个机会提到，你们所做的一切是如何违背曼德拉为他的人民所希望的一切，你们基本上是一个缺乏成就、搞砸一切的国家。所有这些都太棒了。我们衷心感谢你们为这一重要故事情节所做出的个人和集体贡献，并敦促你们继续挥霍你们的潜力。”

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:28.740
<v Ira Glass>Can I say what I love about this? It was written by a journalist named Richard Poplak for the South African news service Daily Maverick, which you can find online.
<v Ira Glass>我能说说我喜欢它的原因吗？它是由一位名叫理查德·波普拉克（Richard Poplak）的记者为南非新闻服务机构“每日小牛”（Daily Maverick）撰写的，你可以在网上找到。

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:50.020
<v Ira Glass>What I love about this is how it doesn't just take us foreigners inside how a South African might see our news coverage and the tone of that coverage, it also reveals a world of utterly justified resentments that I guess maybe we could have suspected, but we didn't really know about. It is an insider's view totally.
<v Ira Glass>我喜欢这篇文章的原因在于，它不仅让我们这些外国人深入了解了南非人如何看待我们的新闻报道以及报道的基调，它还揭示了一个完全合理的怨恨世界，我想也许我们早就怀疑过，但并没有真正了解。这是一个完全内部人的视角。

00:02:50.020 --> 00:03:00.700
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio show, we have stories from three radically different locations. We dive into three very different worlds for the insider's view that only insiders have.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的广播节目中，我们有来自三个截然不同地点的故事。我们深入研究三个非常不同的世界，获取只有内部人士才有的内部视角。

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:15.290
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》（This American Life），由国际公共广播电台（Public Radio International）发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:24.000
<v Ira Glass>Act One, The Weeds of Discontent. What we have now is a recording of a very unusual conversation that came about in an unusual way.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，不满的杂草。我们现在听到的是一段非常不寻常的谈话录音，这段谈话的由来也非同寻常。

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:39.390
<v Ira Glass>There's this filmmaker named Eugene Jarecki who made a documentary about prisons and the criminal justice system called The House I Live In . And he's been taking it around the country and showing it in prisons, kind of Johnny Cash style, for inmates and corrections staff.
<v Ira Glass>有一位名叫尤金·贾瑞奇（Eugene Jarecki）的电影制作人，他拍摄了一部关于监狱和刑事司法系统的纪录片，名为《我居住的房子》（The House I Live In）。他带着这部片子在全国各地巡回，像强尼·卡什（Johnny Cash）那样，在监狱里为囚犯和矫正工作人员放映。

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:45.090
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Brian Reed, went to one of those screenings, which is where this conversation happened. Here's Brian.
<v Ira Glass>我们的制片人之一布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）参加了其中一场放映会，这段对话就是在那儿发生的。下面请听布莱恩的报道。

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:59.360
<v Brian Reed>The screening I saw was at Joseph Harp Correctional Center, a medium security prison in Lexington, Oklahoma. About 35 inmates, most of them in for drug related crimes or with a history of drug use, sat waiting in the visiting room in chairs facing a projector screen.
<v Brian Reed>我看到的放映会是在俄克拉何马州莱克星顿的一所中度安全监狱——约瑟夫·哈普惩教中心（Joseph Harp Correctional Center）举行的。大约35名囚犯，大多数是因为毒品相关犯罪入狱或有吸毒史，坐在探视室的椅子上，面对着投影屏幕。

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.980
<v Prison Guard>Once we get to where we're getting ready to start the movie, if you're here, you're staying.
<v Prison Guard>一旦我们准备开始放映电影，既然你来了，你就得留下来。

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:14.630
<v Brian Reed>More than a dozen staff members came to watch the movie too, including the warden and some state officials from the Oklahoma Department of Corrections. And before the screening started, what did these drug offenders think the movie was going to be about?
<v Brian Reed>十几名工作人员也来看了这部电影，包括监狱长和来自俄克拉何马州惩教署的一些州官员。在放映开始前，这些毒品罪犯认为这部电影是关于什么的呢？

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.610
<v Prisoner 1>Rehab.
<v Prisoner 1>戒毒。

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:21.130
<v Prisoner 2>Probably about some drugs and how to prevent yourself from doing some drugs.
<v Prisoner 2>可能是关于一些毒品，以及如何预防自己不接触毒品。

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:27.620
<v Prisoner 3>How drugs is bad for you. How it mess up your skin and your body. Make you age quicker too.
<v Prisoner 3>毒品对你有多坏。它是如何破坏你的皮肤和身体的。还会让你老得更快。

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:35.620
<v Brian Reed>But these guys were way off base. The House I Live In is about how drugs are bad for them, but not in the way they were expecting.
<v Brian Reed>但这些人完全猜错了。《我居住的房子》确实是关于毒品对他们有多么不好，但并不是他们预期的那种方式。

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:58.620
<v Brian Reed>Because it argues that the war on drugs has completely failed. That it's made law enforcement less effective and overwhelmed prisons. That's it's a self-perpetuating system which preys on poor, uneducated people. A system where politicians win elections by being tough on crime, a multi-billion dollar industry and entire towns thrive off the business of incarceration, and laws trap people with few opportunities in a cycle of committing crimes.
<v Brian Reed>因为它论证了禁毒战争已经彻底失败。它使执法效率降低，并让监狱不堪重负。这是一个自我延续的系统，它掠夺贫穷、未受教育的人。一个政客通过对犯罪强硬来赢得选举的系统，一个价值数十亿美元的产业，整个城镇依靠监禁产业为生，而法律则将几乎没有机会的人困在犯罪的循环中。

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:08.670
<v Charles Ogletree>People don't realize, for example, that when you arrest a young black man and he goes to jail, when he gets out of the prison, he can't get a job in most places because of his record.
<v Charles Ogletree>例如，人们没有意识到，当你逮捕一个年轻的黑人男子，他进了监狱，当他出狱时，因为他的犯罪记录，他在大多数地方都找不到工作。

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.370
<v Brian Reed>This is Charles Ogletree, a professor at Harvard Law School.
<v Brian Reed>这是查尔斯·奥格尔特里（Charles Ogletree），哈佛法学院教授。

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:25.940
<v Charles Ogletree>If he wants to go back to school to go to college, he's ineligible by law for certain grants. He can't get certain health care benefits. His family that was the centerpiece of his life, if they're living in public housing, they can't take him in. He can't vote!
<v Charles Ogletree>如果他想回学校上大学，他依法没有资格获得某些助学金。他无法获得某些医疗保健福利。作为他生活核心的家人，如果他们住在公共住房里，他们不能收留他。他不能投票！

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:38.600
<v Brian Reed>The film has interviews with people throughout the criminal justice system. Inmates and their families, but also judges, police officers, and prison staff. Almost all of them say, in some way or another, that the system needs to be fixed.
<v Brian Reed>这部电影采访了整个刑事司法系统中的人们。包括囚犯和他们的家人，但也包括法官、警察和监狱工作人员。几乎所有人都以这样或那样的方式表示，这个系统需要修复。

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:53.530
<v Brian Reed>And what made it even more interesting for this audience in Oklahoma is that several people from the Oklahoma Department of Corrections are in the movie. And a couple of them were at the screening, watching it with the prisoners. Like Mike Carpenter, who oversees security for Oklahoma's medium security prisons.
<v Brian Reed>对俄克拉何马州的观众来说，更让他们感兴趣的是，俄克拉何马州惩教署的几个人就在电影里。其中几个人就在放映现场，和囚犯们一起观看。比如负责俄克拉何马州中度安全监狱安保工作的迈克·卡彭特（Mike Carpenter）。

00:05:53.530 --> 00:06:10.100
<v Mike Carpenter>I am very much a law and order kind of guy. I would rather have 10 police cars than 1 soup kitchen if you give me the choice. But sometimes I think that we need to be smarter about what those police officers are actually out there doing.
<v Mike Carpenter>我是一个非常崇尚法律和秩序的人。如果让我选择，我宁愿有10辆警车，也不要一个救济食堂。但有时我认为，我们需要对那些警察实际在做什么更加聪明一点。

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:20.940
<v Mike Carpenter>I think sometimes we have people doing a whole lot of time for not very much crime. It's almost like they're paying for our fear, instead of paying for their crime.
<v Mike Carpenter>我想有时人们因为并不严重的犯罪而服刑很久。这简直就像他们在为我们的恐惧买单，而不是为他们的罪行买单。

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:34.420
<v Brian Reed>So when the screening was over, some of the inmates were surprised that prison staff had even shown them this at all. One guy said it was exhilarating to hear corrections officers say things like, "We're jailing too many people. We're locking them up for too long."
<v Brian Reed>所以当放映结束时，一些囚犯很惊讶监狱工作人员竟然会给他们看这个。一个人说，听到矫正官说出“我们关押的人太多了。我们把他们关得太久了”这样的话，真是令人振奋。

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:49.560
<v Brian Reed>So we had this idea. We thought the film might prompt a conversation that prisoners and guards don't normally get to have about the prison system. And Oklahoma corrections authorities agreed to give us a room where I could sit down prisoners and guards and they could talk to each other one on one.
<v Brian Reed>所以我们有了这个主意。我们认为这部电影可能会促成一场囚犯和狱警通常无法进行的关于监狱系统的对话。俄克拉何马州惩教当局同意给我们一个房间，让我可以让囚犯和狱警坐下来一对一地谈话。

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.480
<v Brian Reed>Who do you want to talk to? Who do you want to talk with?
<v Brian Reed>你想和谁谈？你想和谁交流？

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.550
<v Antwaun Wells>I want to talk to Dooley and Shelock.
<v Antwaun Wells>我想和杜利（Dooley）和谢洛克（Shelock）谈。

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:58.720
<v Brian Reed>OK. Why do you want to talk to them?
<v Brian Reed>好的。你为什么想和他们谈？

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:01.520
<v Antwaun Wells>My own personal opinions.
<v Antwaun Wells>我个人的看法。

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:14.760
<v Brian Reed>This is Antwaun Wells. He's 25, serving the third year of a nine year sentence for shooting a guy during an argument and also for stealing TVs from a Target and then stabbing a security guard in the arm on the way out of the store.
<v Brian Reed>这是安特万·威尔斯（Antwaun Wells）。他25岁，正在服九年刑期的第三年，罪名是在争执中射伤了一个人，还在从塔吉特（Target）百货商店出来时偷了电视机，并刺伤了一名保安的手臂。

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:23.500
<v Brian Reed>It's not his first time in prison. He'd been in before. One of the staff members he picked-- Shelock-- wasn't around. But Dooley was.
<v Brian Reed>这不是他第一次进监狱。他之前进去过。他选的工作人员之一——谢洛克——不在。但杜利在。

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:35.880
<v Brian Reed>Dooley is Lt. Cecil Dooley. He's been a corrections officer for 14 years. He's a big burly guy. He wasn't there to see the movie. He was the guard on duty supervising the inmates as they watched the film.
<v Brian Reed>杜利是塞西尔·杜利中尉（Lt. Cecil Dooley）。他当矫正官已经14年了。他是个身材魁梧的家伙。他不是来看电影的。他是当班的守卫，负责监督囚犯看电影。

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:39.880
<v Brian Reed>I recorded other conversations throughout the day. But this was the one that stood out.
<v Brian Reed>我在这一天录制了其他对话。但这一个是表现最突出的。

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:44.770
<v Brian Reed>We're going to go in here. Pardon me, guys. We got our first pair. We'll see how this goes.
<v Brian Reed>我们要进去这里。抱歉，伙计们。我们有了第一对。看看进展如何。

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:51.120
<v Ira Glass>Lt. Dooley and Antwaun sat down facing each other. I sat off to the side to try and stay out of their way.
<v Ira Glass>杜利中尉和安特万面对面坐着。我坐在旁边，尽量不碍他们的事。

00:07:51.120 --> 00:08:01.200
<v Ira Glass>Now let me say here that this whole thing was a total experiment. I don't think any of us knew what to expect. And just to get in front of this, Antwaun did not bring Dooley in here to talk about the movie.
<v Ira Glass>现在让我说，这一切完全是一个实验。我想我们都没人知道会发生什么。为了先发制人，安特万找杜利进来不是为了谈电影。

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.820
<v Brian Reed>All right, so you wanted to bring him in here and ask him a question.
<v Brian Reed>好的，所以你想把他叫进来问他一个问题。

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:04.980
<v Antwaun Wells>Yep.
<v Antwaun Wells>是的。

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:05.390
<v Brian Reed>Do you have your question?
<v Brian Reed>你有你的问题了吗？

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:11.990
<v Antwaun Wells>I want to know why y'all feel that it's so important for y'all to demand respect, but don't want to give it.
<v Antwaun Wells>我想知道为什么你们觉得要求得到尊重那么重要，却不想给予别人尊重。

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:17.120
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>When you say "y'all," you putting everybody in the same boat.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>当你把“你们”放在一起说时，你是把所有人都归为一类了。

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:29.660
<v Antwaun Wells>Well, I can't put everyone in the same boat, because there is some guards that are respectful. But it's some-- being you in particular with me-- I feel that you're just disrespectful.
<v Antwaun Wells>嗯，我不能把每个人都归为一类，因为确实有一些狱警是尊重的。但有一些——特别是你对我——我觉得你只是不尊重人。

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:38.490
<v Antwaun Wells>Like the other day in the office, I asked you to come here. If you was to ask me to come here and I told you to hold on, you'd have wrote me up because I didn't follow a direct order.
<v Antwaun Wells>就像前几天在办公室，我让你来这里。如果你让我来这里，而我说等一下，你就会写报告举报我，因为我没有遵循直接命令。

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:41.020
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I acknowledged you. I told you to hold on.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我回应你了。我让你等一下。

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.090
<v Antwaun Wells>I [INAUDIBLE].
<v Antwaun Wells>我[听不清]。

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:48.720
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Unless you're bleeding, dying, or giving birth, the stuff that I had going on was much more important than what you needed at that time.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>除非你在流血、垂死或正在分娩，否则我手头的事比你当时需要的要重要得多。

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:49.340
<v Antwaun Wells>I just feel--
<v Antwaun Wells>我只是觉得——

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.520
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I've never once called you outside of your name.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我从来没有叫过你的名字之外的称呼。

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:51.900
<v Antwaun Wells>No, that's--
<v Antwaun Wells>不，那是——

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:54.020
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I've never slandered you in any way.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我从来没有以任何方式诽谤过你。

00:08:54.020 --> 00:09:00.090
<v Antwaun Wells>Yeah. No. And I'm not gonna say that and I never will say that incident happened. But what I'm saying is that's not the only--
<v Antwaun Wells>是的。不。我不会说那个，我永远也不会说那个事件发生了。但我想说的是那不是唯一——

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.810
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>But you come in here and you say I was disrespectful because you don't get what you want when you want it.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>但你走进来，说我不尊重人，因为你没能在想要的时候得到你想要的。

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:13.750
<v Antwaun Wells>In my eyes, I feel like you and other guards view us as if we're nothing. Y'all walk round with y'all head held up. You know what I'm saying?
<v Antwaun Wells>在我眼里，我觉得你和其他守卫把我们视为无物。你们走来走去，昂着头。你明白我的意思吗？

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:24.440
<v Antwaun Wells>It's always, y'all win. No matter what the situation is, y'all win. You have a arrogant attitude. Your attitude is I'm Dooley. I'm big bad Dooley.
<v Antwaun Wells>总是你们赢。无论情况如何，都是你们赢。你有一种傲慢的态度。你的态度就是“我是杜利”。我是那个厉害的大块头杜利。

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:37.960
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>If I tell you to do something that's not illegal, that's not going to jeopardize your life, that's not offensive, you should have no problem doing it. Whether you like it or not is a totally different story.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>如果我让你做某件不违法、不会危及生命、也不冒犯人的事，你应该没有问题去执行。不管你喜不喜欢，那是完全不同的故事。

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:40.780
<v Antwaun Wells>That's where the disrespect come in. Because you had--
<v Antwaun Wells>那才是不尊重人的地方。因为你曾——

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:41.360
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>That's not disrespectful.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>那不叫不尊重人。

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.280
<v Antwaun Wells>OK, you had me pick up dandelions.
<v Antwaun Wells>好的，你让我捡蒲公英。

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:44.090
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Yes.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>是的。

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:48.490
<v Antwaun Wells>Just to prove a point to me. To prove a point that y'all have power.
<v Antwaun Wells>只是为了向我证明一个观点。为了证明你们拥有权力的观点。

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:51.150
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>OK. So you're still upset over the dandelion deal?
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>好的。所以你还在为蒲公英的事生气？

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.000
<v Antwaun Wells>Yes, I really am. That really actually frustrated me.
<v Antwaun Wells>是的，我确实很生气。那确实让我很沮丧。

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.630
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>If you wouldn't have bucked up against that officer that day--
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>如果你那天没有跟那个官员作对——

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:59.200
<v Antwaun Wells>But see, that's what I'm saying. You never came and asked me exactly what happened.
<v Antwaun Wells>但你看，这就是我要说的。你从来没有来问我到底发生了什么。

00:09:59.200 --> 00:09:59.830
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Don't need to.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>不需要。

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:06.880
<v Antwaun Wells>Exactly. You took your officer's word and you left it at that. It's always three sides to each story. My side, his side, and the truth.
<v Antwaun Wells>正是。你听信了你属下的话，就这样结束了。每个故事都有三个方面。我的方面，他的方面，还有真相。

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:13.430
<v Antwaun Wells>You left two sides out and you went off of what your officer said. You never came to me as a man and asked me. You know what I'm saying?
<v Antwaun Wells>你忽略了两个方面，完全根据你属下说的话行事。你从来没有像个男人一样来问我。你明白我的意思吗？

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:13.890
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I did.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我问过。

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:19.020
<v Antwaun Wells>And then you forced me to do something just because you could. And if I didn't, you threatened to ship me.
<v Antwaun Wells>然后你强迫我做某事仅仅因为你能。如果我不做，你威胁要把我转走。

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.710
<v Brian Reed>Antwaun is saying Lt. Dooley threatened to ship him to another prison.
<v Brian Reed>安特万是说杜利中尉威胁要把他转到另一所监狱。

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:25.690
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I don't remember threatening to ship you. But I could.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我不记得威胁要转走你。但我可以。

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:26.580
<v Antwaun Wells>Yes, you threatened to ship me.
<v Antwaun Wells>是的，你威胁要转走我。

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:27.830
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I could. Disobedience to order.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我可以。抗命。

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:30.180
<v Antwaun Wells>Exactly. And you told me trying to override your staff, you could ship me.
<v Antwaun Wells>正是。你告诉我，试图越过你的员工，你就可以转走我。

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:31.380
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Mm hmm. I could.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>嗯嗯。我可以。

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.580
<v Antwaun Wells>And if I didn't pick up dandelions,  you'll put me in a holding cell.
<v Antwaun Wells>如果我不捡蒲公英，你就会把我关进牢房。

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:42.150
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>And if I remember correctly, that was over you trying to get other people to stand up against that officer and not do what he was telling you to do.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>如果我没记错的话，那是因为你试图让其他人站出来反抗那个官员，不要做他告诉你们做的事情。

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:42.840
<v Antwaun Wells>No. That's exactly what I'm  saying.
<v Antwaun Wells>不。那正是我想说的。

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:49.750
<v Brian Reed>So OK, sure. They weren't talking about the history of the drug war or the prison industrial complex or any of the other big ideas from the movie.
<v Brian Reed>好的，没问题。他们没有谈论禁毒战争的历史、监狱工业复合体或电影中任何其他宏大的想法。

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:58.960
<v Brian Reed>But it was interesting to listen to them argue like this. It was intimate. It was personal. They were bickering like family members. Like a dad and his kid going at it.
<v Brian Reed>但听他们这样争论很有意思。这是亲密的。这是私人的。他们像家庭成员一样争吵。就像父亲和孩子在争执。

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:14.980
<v Brian Reed>And I felt like I was seeing something people on the outside don't usually have the opportunity to see. You realize, oh, right, these two are embedded in each other's lives in a way that only happens when one person has the power to punish the other and the other person has a whole lot of time to stew over it.
<v Brian Reed>我觉得我看到了外界人们通常没有机会看到的东西。你意识到，哦，对了，这两个人以一种只有在一个人有权力惩罚另一个人，而另一个人有大量时间去为之烦恼时才会发生的方式，嵌入了彼此的生活。

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:25.800
<v Brian Reed>And the thing that was bugging Antwaun, the dandelions, seems small, but kind of gets to the heart of what he hates about being incarcerated. And he wanted to hash that out with Dooley.
<v Brian Reed>而困扰安特万的那件事——蒲公英，看起来很小，但触及了他讨厌被监禁的核心。他想和杜利把这件事理清楚。

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:42.200
<v Brian Reed>Obviously, there's disagreement about what happened that day, which was months ago, by the way. Antwaun says another officer had given some other inmates a few tasks to do, like pick up trash. Antwaun went up to the other inmates and told them they were allowed to do the tasks in a different order than the guard did.
<v Brian Reed>显然，对于那天发生的事情，也就是几个月前的事，存在分歧。安特万说另一名官员给其他囚犯布置了一些任务，比如捡垃圾。安特万走到其他囚犯那里，告诉他们可以按不同于守卫的顺序完成任务。

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:51.990
<v Brian Reed>Dooley says that's not how it happened. That Antwaun Wells was telling other inmates to disobey a guard. So Dooley gave him what they call extra duty. In this case, to pick dandelions.
<v Brian Reed>杜利说事情不是这样发生的。安特万·威尔斯当时在教唆其他囚犯违抗守卫。所以杜利给了他所谓的额外任务。在这种情况下，就是捡蒲公英。

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:52.720
<v Antwaun Wells>I don't--
<v Antwaun Wells>我不——

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:53.520
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Was it hard?
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>很难吗？

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.590
<v Antwaun Wells>No. I mean, it wasn't hard. It was just--
<v Antwaun Wells>不。我是说，那并不难。只是——

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:56.350
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Did it take a lot of time?
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>花了很多时间吗？

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.290
<v Antwaun Wells>No. It didn't take a lot of time. It's still the principle. You made me--
<v Antwaun Wells>不。并没有花很多时间。这仍然是原则问题。你强迫我——

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:00.560
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Did you break any bones?
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>你骨折了吗？

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:06.650
<v Antwaun Wells>No. You could have said, go pick up some trash. You specifically made me pick up a flower that grows out the ground.
<v Antwaun Wells>没有。你可以说，去捡些垃圾。你特别让我去捡地上长的花。

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:09.010
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Mm hmm.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>嗯嗯。

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:11.000
<v Antwaun Wells>Just because you could.
<v Antwaun Wells>仅仅因为你能。

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:11.630
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Right.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>对。

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:20.780
<v Antwaun Wells>Exactly. It's always going to be officers that feel like just because I have the power and authority, I can do and say what I want to to these people.
<v Antwaun Wells>正是。总是会有官员觉得仅仅因为我有权力和权威，我就可以对这些人做我想做的事，说我想说的话。

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:28.130
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>There wasn't just to put power and authority. I am where I am because I have morals, ethics, and beliefs that I stand by.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>那不仅仅是为了行使权力和权威。我之所以在现在的位置，是因为我有我所坚持的道德、伦理和信念。

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:29.380
<v Antwaun Wells>Still yet even once again--
<v Antwaun Wells>即便如此，再一次——

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:37.220
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I think your problem, Mr. Wells, is you like to dwell on the fact that you don't get your way each and every time. And that's part of the reason that you're in prison--
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我想你的问题是，威尔斯先生，你喜欢纠结于你并没有每次都如愿以偿的事实。这也是你入狱的部分原因——

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:37.890
<v Antwaun Wells>No, it's not.
<v Antwaun Wells>不，不是的。

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.340
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>--is because you like to buck the system.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>——是因为你喜欢反抗体制。

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:42.880
<v Antwaun Wells>I'm in prison 'cause I shot somebody. I'll never deny that fact.
<v Antwaun Wells>我入狱是因为我射伤了人。我永远不会否认这个事实。

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:43.200
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>OK.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>好的。

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.320
<v Antwaun Wells>I'm in prison because I put myself here.
<v Antwaun Wells>我入狱是因为我把自己送进来的。

00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:45.460
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>OK.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>好的。

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.630
<v Antwaun Wells>I made the choices in my life because I chose to.
<v Antwaun Wells>我做出生活中的选择是因为我选择了这么做。

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:48.540
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>And those are poor decisions.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>而那些是糟糕的决定。

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:55.880
<v Antwaun Wells>I don't blame nobody for nothing that happens. But when I feel that's just like there's some guards here that's just rude for no reason. I don't understand that.
<v Antwaun Wells>我不责怪任何人发生的事情。但是当我感觉到这里有一些守卫毫无理由地粗鲁时。我不明白这一点。

00:12:55.880 --> 00:13:05.410
<v Antwaun Wells>You didn't care enough about me to even ask me my opinion. You didn't care enough about nothing that I had going on, once again, because I'm an inmate. Y'all feel just because I came to the penitentiary, that I have no rights.
<v Antwaun Wells>你根本不在乎我，连问我的意见都不问。你根本不在乎我正在经历什么，再一次，因为我是一名囚犯。你们觉得仅仅因为我进了监狱，我就没有权利。

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.250
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>You being an offender has nothing to do with it. 'Cause you have no say over anything.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>你是罪犯与此无关。因为你对任何事情都没有发言权。

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:09.160
<v Antwaun Wells>Policy and procedure.
<v Antwaun Wells>政策和程序。

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:13.140
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>You have no say to tell my officer what he can and cannot do, to ask people to do.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>你没有发言权去告诉我的官员他可以做什么，不可以做什么，去要求别人做什么。

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:18.450
<v Antwaun Wells>Exactly. That's the point where you're missing. I told the inmates that. I didn't tell the officer nothing.
<v Antwaun Wells>正是。这就是你忽略的地方。我告诉囚犯们了。我没对那个官员说什么。

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:21.550
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Right. Which makes you the person that was instigating.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>对。这让你成了挑起事端的人。

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:23.870
<v Brian Reed>They went on like this for more than 20 minutes.
<v Brian Reed>他们就这样谈了20多分钟。

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:26.630
<v Antwaun Wells>I know you don't like me, just like I don't like you.
<v Antwaun Wells>我知道你不喜欢我，就像我不喜欢你一样。

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:29.670
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>I've never disrespected you and my job has nothing to do with like or dislike.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>我从来没有不尊重你，我的工作与喜欢或不喜欢无关。

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:33.540
<v Antwaun Wells>You disrespected me that day that you didn't ask me my side of the story and you made me pick up dandelions--
<v Antwaun Wells>你那天不听我的辩解，强迫我捡蒲公英，你就是不尊重我——

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.220
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>That's not disrespect.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>那不叫不尊重。

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:34.910
<v Antwaun Wells>Because you could.
<v Antwaun Wells>因为你能。

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:35.480
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>That's not disrespect.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>那不叫不尊重。

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:38.660
<v Antwaun Wells>You power abused because you could. You just admitted it.
<v Antwaun Wells>你滥用权力，因为你能。你刚刚承认了。

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:39.470
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>It's not even abuse of power.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>那甚至不是滥用权力。

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:40.330
<v Antwaun Wells>OK.
<v Antwaun Wells>好的。

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:49.190
<v Brian Reed>Finally I interrupted and asked them what they thought about the movie we just watched. Dooley had actually seen it before. He had it recorded on his DVR. And he liked it.
<v Brian Reed>最后我插话问他们对我们刚看的电影有什么看法。杜利实际上之前看过。他录在DVR里了。他很喜欢。

00:13:49.190 --> 00:14:00.370
<v Brian Reed>He told me that for several years now, he's felt that the country needs to rethink the way it does corrections. He said he often tells inmates that when they get down to the last three years or so of their sentence, he thinks they should be released early.
<v Brian Reed>他告诉我，几年来他一直觉得国家需要重新思考矫正方式。他说他经常告诉囚犯，当他们服刑只剩下最后三年左右时，他认为他们应该提前获释。

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:07.610
<v Brian Reed>When you see that movie and when you think about your day to day, are you dissatisfied? Are you frustrated with the system, where it's gotten?
<v Brian Reed>当你看到那部电影，当你想到你的日常生活时，你感到不满吗？你对这个系统、对它所达到的地步感到沮丧吗？

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:08.410
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Absolutely. Absolutely.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>绝对，绝对。

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.970
<v Brian Reed>What frustrates you most?
<v Brian Reed>什么最让你沮丧？

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:29.480
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>The fact that we have now taken people and made them a monetary commodity. Right now the big thing is the push to privatization. To make money off of prisons, to make money off of people's lives, to make money to incarcerate people. That to me, seems backwards.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>事实是我们现在已经把人变成了货币商品。现在最大的是推动私有化。从监狱中赚钱，从人们的生活中赚钱，从监禁人们中赚钱。这对我来说似乎是倒退的。

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.420
<v Brian Reed>He turned to Antwaun for a second.
<v Brian Reed>他转向安特万一下。

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:53.030
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>And this is not to push your button or anything like that. I did more in 10 seconds of his life by making him pick up dandelions. He will remember the fact that I came up there and I told him to pick dandelions for arguing with an officer. He will remember that forever.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>这不是为了刺激你什么的。我用那10秒钟让他捡蒲公英，对他的人生产生的影响更多。他会记得我走到那里告诉他因为和官员争吵而捡蒲公英的事实。他会永远记得。

00:14:53.030 --> 00:15:07.070
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>And if that one issue makes him hate me to the point where he doesn't ever want to put himself in that position again, then I have done my job. I've done my job to correct an action forever.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>如果这一个问题让他恨我到他再也不想把自己置于那种境地，那么我就完成了我的工作。我完成了纠正一个行为的工作，永远地。

00:15:07.070 --> 00:15:10.570
<v Brian Reed>What do you think of that, Antwaun? You get that?
<v Brian Reed>安特万，你怎么看？你明白吗？

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:14.740
<v Antwaun Wells>I mean, I understand it. But I still don't feel that it's fair.
<v Antwaun Wells>我是说，我理解。但我仍然觉得那不公平。

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:28.550
<v Antwaun Wells>Penitentiary to me is just slavery in another form. In my eyes, these is my owners and I have to do what they say, otherwise I have consequences. Just like the slave master. You either do what he say or you get punished.
<v Antwaun Wells>对我来说，监狱只是另一种形式的奴隶制。在我眼里，这些人是我的主人，我必须做他们说的，否则我会有后果。就像奴隶主。你要么做他说的，要么你就会受到惩罚。

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:39.850
<v Brian Reed>Then the conversation got more personal. Lt. Dooley told Antwaun that the last time he got out of prison he had a chance to stay out, to make a different life for himself. But instead he re-offended.
<v Brian Reed>然后对话变得更加私密。杜利中尉告诉安特万，他上次出狱时有机会留在外面，过上不同的生活。但他反而再次犯案。

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:46.680
<v Brian Reed>Antwaun said he didn't have many options. And it was around this point that Antwaun and Dooley actually started to hear each other out a bit.
<v Brian Reed>安特万说他没有太多选择。就是在这一点上，安特万和杜利实际上开始听取对方的一点意见了。

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:50.600
<v Antwaun Wells>What was I supposed to do? I come from the hood. I come from nothing. You tell me, what was I supposed to do?
<v Antwaun Wells>我能做什么？我来自贫民区。我一无所有。你告诉我，我能做什么？

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.230
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>Now you're talking outside my purview, because I don't know where you're from.
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>现在你说的超出了我的职权范围，因为我不知道你是哪里人。

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:58.620
<v Antwaun Wells>Exactly. Exactly. You don't know where I'm from. And I don't feel that you would care.
<v Antwaun Wells>正是。正是。你不知道我是哪里人。而且我觉得你也不会在乎。

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:10.840
<v Antwaun Wells>Me as a person, if I didn't have all the tattoos or whatever, if I had the money to get them removed, you could put me in a white collar neighborhood and I could make it because I have a education. You know what I'm saying? I have two years of college.
<v Antwaun Wells>作为我个人，如果我没有所有的纹身什么的，如果有钱把它们去掉，你可以把我放在白领社区，我能成功，因为我有教育。你明白我的意思吗？我有两年的大学学历。

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:17.510
<v Antwaun Wells>I have no other option but to accept my choices I made. You know what I'm saying? I do admit my failures. You know what  I'm saying?
<v Antwaun Wells>除了接受我所做的选择，我别无选择。你明白我的意思吗？我确实承认我的失败。你明白我的意思吗？

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:24.960
<v Antwaun Wells>But I will say that there were obstacles that at that time, I didn't think right of overcoming, so I chose the wrong way to overcome them.
<v Antwaun Wells>但我会说，当时有一些我没认为有权克服的障碍，所以我选择了错误的克服方式。

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:38.340
<v Lt. Cecil Dooley>You know what? I grew up very poor. I didn't know I was poor. My mom always had two different jobs growing up. I was taking care of my little brother at the age of eight by myself.
