00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:47.160
<v Ira Glass>At times I feel so caught up in immigration. And the idea that we've been slighted in some way.
<v Ira Glass>有时我觉得如此被移民所困。以及我们被以某种方式轻视的想法。

00:36:47.160 --> 00:37:09.890
<v Ira Glass>And lately, I don't really feel that way. My husband did break the law and this is the result of laws that were put in place with good intentions. And now what I want to concentrate on is my life here.
<v Ira Glass>最近，我真的不那样觉得了。我的丈夫确实违反了法律，这是以善意实施的法律的结果。现在我想专注于我在这里的生活。

00:37:09.890 --> 00:37:27.100
<v Ira Glass>This is my life. I can't change the law. I mean, I marched in protest rallies and I was involved in immigration reform and I was a member of different groups and talked to congressmen. And I did all those things for all these years while we were in the US.
<v Ira Glass>这是我的生活。我不能改变法律。我是说，我参加了抗议集会，我参与了移民改革，我曾是不同团体的成员，也和国会议员交谈过。我在美国时多年来做了所有那些事情。

00:37:27.100 --> 00:37:42.970
<v Ira Glass>And when we got here, it was just like a weight had been lifted off my shoulder. Because I didn't have to [INAUDIBLE] deal with any of that anymore. I didn't have to think about my husband getting picked up by ICE on our way to church or whatever.
<v Ira Glass>当我们来到这里时，就像一个重担从我的肩上卸了下来。因为我不必[听不清]再处理任何那些事情了。我不必考虑我丈夫在我们去教堂的路上被ICE带走之类的事。

00:37:42.970 --> 00:38:00.220
<v Ira Glass>I mean, the most simple, mundane activities in the US always had to involve some thought about immigration. And it might be a cop out just because I don't want to talk about immigration. I don't want to think about it. I don't want to argue with anybody that it's not fair. I just don't feel like doing it.
<v Ira Glass>我是说，在美国最简单、平凡的活动总是必须涉及一些关于移民的想法。而这可能是一个借口，仅仅因为我不想谈论移民。我不想去想它。我不想和任何人争论它不公平。我只是不想做它。

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:18.250
<v Ira Glass>I'm tired. I'm really, really tired. You know. And I don't know. Goodnight.
<v Ira Glass>我累了。我真的，真的累了。你知道。我不知道。晚安。

00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:28.850
<v Ira Glass>This feels extremely too loud for what time it is. It's 5:45. I take Gordo to work first.
<v Ira Glass>这感觉对现在的时间来说太吵了。是5:45。我先送Gordo去上班。

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:30.340
<v Raymundo Cruz>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Raymundo Cruz>[说西班牙语]

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:40.030
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[LAUGHS] He says he doesn't talk in the mornings. He's making fun of me because I don't talk in the mornings. I hate talking in the morning.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[笑] 他说他在早晨不说话。他在取笑我，因为我在早晨不说话。我讨厌在早晨说话。

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:52.490
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>So we're on Hermanos Escobar now. And we're behind a line of buses. They're all going to a maquila somewhere.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>所以我们现在在Hermanos Escobar。我们在排队的公交车后面。它们都在去某个地方的马基拉。

00:38:52.490 --> 00:39:20.580
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>When I look at people going in and out of the maquilas, I just see a little dollar sign on everyone's forehead. Because they most certainly make a ton of money off these poor people who are working for-- god, I don't even know what they make. It's like $0.80 an hour or something that he makes. I don't even think it's that, actually. What?
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>当我看着人们进进出出马基拉时，我只是看到每个人的额头上有一个小美元符号。因为他们最肯定是从这些工作的人身上赚了成吨的钱，工作为了——天哪，我甚至不知道他们赚多少。就像每小时0.80美元或者是他赚的什么。我甚至不认为实际上是那样。什么？

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:22.300
<v Raymundo Cruz>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Raymundo Cruz>[说西班牙语]

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:24.590
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[说西班牙语]

00:39:24.590 --> 00:39:25.690
<v Raymundo Cruz>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Raymundo Cruz>[说西班牙语]

00:39:25.690 --> 00:39:26.170
<v Woman>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Woman>[说西班牙语]

00:39:26.170 --> 00:39:30.730
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[SPEAKING SPANISH ANGRILY]
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[愤怒地用西班牙语说]

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:37.570
<v Raymundo Cruz>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Raymundo Cruz>[说西班牙语]

00:39:37.570 --> 00:39:43.590
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>And thankfully, we're dropping him off. Have a good day!
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>值得庆幸的是，我们要送他下车了。祝你有美好的一天！

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:44.370
<v Raymundo Cruz>Bye!
<v Raymundo Cruz>拜！

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:45.290
<v Raymundo Cruz>[CAR DOOR SLAMS]
<v Raymundo Cruz>[车门关上]

00:39:45.290 --> 00:39:52.970
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[LAUGHS] We're so not morning people. We'll have the most horrible fights in the morning.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[笑] 我们真不是早起的人。我们会在早晨进行最可怕的争吵。

00:39:52.970 --> 00:40:06.840
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>So I just dropped him off at the maquila. And he'll work there all day long. He makes the harnesses for American cars.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>所以我刚刚把他送到马基拉。他整天都会在那里工作。他制造美国汽车的线束。

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:38.430
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>And now we're going to the bridge. There is literally no line. Oh, I'm so excited. The line can go back a couple miles on a normal day, but I'm pulling right up onto the bridge. And I'm here. [YAWNS]
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>现在我们要去桥那里了。真的没有队伍。哦，我太激动了。在正常的一天，队伍可以排到几英里远，但我正拉上桥。我到了。[打哈欠]

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:53.940
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>The basic routine is first texting my husband. At the end of the day, this is Juarez and we have to make sure I didn't die. I mean, seriously.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>基本的常规是先发短信给我丈夫。在一天结束时，这是华雷斯，我们必须确保我没死。我是说，真的。

00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:55.370
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[BEEP]
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>[哔]

00:40:55.370 --> 00:41:10.470
<v Ira Glass>Gordo just texted me back to have a good day. [LAUGHS] We really just should not be talking to each other in the morning. It's detrimental to everything.
<v Ira Glass>Gordo刚刚回信祝我有个美好的一天。[笑] 我们真的不应该在早晨互相交谈。这对一切都有害。

00:41:10.470 --> 00:41:27.280
<v Ira Glass>So basic routine is texting. And then makeup, social networking, maybe social networking, makeup, social networking. It's pretty basic. Mascara.
<v Ira Glass>所以基本的常规是发短信。然后化妆，社交网络，也许社交网络，化妆，社交网络。很基本。睫毛膏。

00:41:27.280 --> 00:41:30.760
<v Ira Glass>[TAPPING]
<v Ira Glass>[敲击]

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:52.090
<v Ira Glass>Eyeshadow. OK, I'm still doing my makeup. I'm almost about to cross already, though. It's always kind of fun to see the new agents confused about the white lady.
<v Ira Glass>眼影。好的，我还在化妆。虽然我快要过境了。看到新的特工对那个白人女士感到困惑总是很有趣。

00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:52.240
<v Ira Glass>Morning!
<v Ira Glass>早安！

00:41:52.240 --> 00:41:53.800
<v Border Agent>Morning, ma'am. Where you going today?
<v Border Agent>早安，女士。你今天去哪儿？

00:41:53.800 --> 00:41:55.490
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>Work.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>工作。

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:56.830
<v Border Agent>What are you bringing from Mexico?
<v Border Agent>你从墨西哥带了什么？

00:41:56.830 --> 00:42:00.440
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>Nothing.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>什么都没有。

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:01.390
<v Border Agent>Have a good day.
<v Border Agent>祝你有美好的一天。

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:03.290
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>Thank you. You too.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>谢谢。你也一样。

00:42:03.290 --> 00:42:05.080
<v Border Agent>Thank you.
<v Border Agent>谢谢。

00:42:05.080 --> 00:42:25.190
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>And that was that. So it's 6:21 and I don't go to work until 8 o'clock. So now I have like an hour and a half before I really [YAWNS] need to be at work. So I'm going to go to sleep. [LAUGHS]
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>就是这样。现在是6:21，我直到8点才上班。所以我现在有大约一个半小时，在我真的[打哈欠]需要去上班之前。所以我打算去睡觉。[笑]

00:42:25.190 --> 00:42:38.530
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>This is what I do. You have only a handful of options. You can go to Walmart, which is a very popular option. You can go to McDonald's, another very popular option. Or you can go to sleep in your car.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>这就是我做的。你只有少数选择。你可以去沃尔玛，这是一个非常受欢迎的选择。你可以去麦当劳，另一个非常受欢迎的选择。或者你可以去你的车里睡觉。

00:42:38.530 --> 00:42:51.430
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>And that, my friends, is what I'm going to do. And I have a little handful of parking lots that I frequent. But my favorite is this golf course by my work. It's really quiet and there's a public pool there.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>而那，我的朋友，就是我打算做的。我有几个我经常去的停车场。但我最喜欢的是我工作旁边的这个高尔夫球场。它真的很安静，那里有一个公共游泳池。

00:42:51.430 --> 00:43:10.310
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>And I think there's a swimming class at 6:30. But it lasts a couple of hours. So I wonder if they think I'm homeless. I think about things  like that way too much.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>我想6:30有一个游泳课。但它持续几个小时。所以我想知道他们是否认为我无家可归。我想那样的事情想太多了。

00:43:10.310 --> 00:43:24.730
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>It's hard to say what Juarez really means to me. It's everything for us. To me, Juarez is the perfect combination of heaven and hell on Earth.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>很难说华雷斯对我真正意味着什么。它是我们的一切。对我来说，华雷斯是地球上天堂和地狱的完美结合。

00:43:24.730 --> 00:43:57.020
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>Despite this raging drug war going on, despite all of the bad things in the city, it's the first place that's ever opened its arms to my husband and I, the first place we can both legally call home. And it's our own little paradise. [YAWNS] [LAUGHS] So tired.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>尽管发生了这场猖獗的毒品战争，尽管城市里有所有糟糕的事情，这是第一个向我和我丈夫张开双臂的地方，第一个我们都能合法地称之为家的地方。这是我们自己的小天堂。[打哈欠] [笑] 太累了。

00:43:57.020 --> 00:44:10.280
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>OK, so I'm going to go to my little golf course and fall asleep. I have my pillow and my blanket that I keep in the car. And that's that.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>好的，所以我打算去我的小高尔夫球场睡觉了。我有我放在车里的枕头和毯子。就是这样。

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:27.570
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>Oh my gosh, what a beautiful sunrise. Gosh.
<v Emily Bonderer Cruz>哦天哪，多么美丽的日出。天哪。

00:44:27.570 --> 00:44:32.390
<v Ira Glass>Emily Bonderer Cruz. Her radio diary was produced by Ann Heppermann with help from Debbie Nathan.
<v Ira Glass>艾米丽·邦德勒·克鲁兹。她的广播日记由安·赫伯曼在黛比·内森（Debbie Nathan）的帮助下制作。

00:44:32.390 --> 00:44:48.430
<v Ira Glass>To read Emily's blog, go to therealhousewifeofcuidadjuarez.blogspot.com. Emily has chosen some favorite posts from her site that we linked to from our website, thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>要阅读艾米丽的博客，请前往therealhousewifeofcuidadjuarez.blogspot.com。艾米丽从她的网站上挑选了一些最喜欢的帖子，我们在我们的网站thisamericanlife.org上链接了这些帖子。

00:44:48.430 --> 00:45:03.620
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Moving On Up. In Israel there's this writer named Sayed Kashua who's a prolific cultural force in that country. He's a novelist. He writes a popular, very funny TV show, where the main character is based on him. And he has a weekly newspaper column.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，向上移动。在以色列有一位名叫赛义德·卡什亚（Sayed Kashua）的作家，他是该国多产的文化力量。他是一位小说家。他写了一部流行、非常有趣的电视节目，其中的主角就是以他为原型的。他还有一个每周报纸专栏。

00:45:03.620 --> 00:45:15.210
<v Ira Glass>And his newspaper columns are basically this very frank, very entertaining conversation he's been having with the entire country for years now. They're about his day-to-day life. Picking up his kids, buying furniture, stuff like that.
<v Ira Glass>他的报纸专栏基本上是他多年来一直与整个国家进行的非常坦率、非常有趣的对话。它们是关于他的日常生活。接他的孩子，买家具，之类的东西。

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:25.860
<v Ira Glass>And while the columns are not explicitly political, he's a minority in Israel. He's an Arab who is also an Israeli citizen. Which, as you might expect, is complicated.
<v Ira Glass>虽然这些专栏不是明确的政治性的，但他以色列的少数派。他是一名也是以色列公民的阿拉伯人。这，正如你可能预料的，是复杂的。

00:45:25.860 --> 00:45:39.260
<v Ira Glass>A few years ago, Kashua and his wife and kids moved from East Jerusalem-- where most of the Arabs in the city live-- to West Jerusalem, where it's almost all Jews, no Arabs. And that kind of blew people's minds, including his.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，卡什亚和他的妻子以及孩子从东耶路撒冷——该市大多数阿拉伯人居住的地方——搬到了西耶路撒冷，那里几乎全是犹太人，没有阿拉伯人。这让人们感到震惊，包括他自己。

00:45:39.260 --> 00:45:53.520
<v Ira Glass>He and his wife made the move to get a better apartment in a neighborhood that had better services. But the move was radical in a country where even middle class and rich Arab Israelis do not usually live among Jews, especially in Jerusalem.
<v Ira Glass>他和他的妻子搬家是为了在设施更好的社区获得更好的公寓。但这种搬迁在一个即使中产阶级和富裕的以色列阿拉伯人通常也不住在一起的地方，尤其是在耶路撒冷，是激进的。

00:45:53.520 --> 00:46:05.010
<v Ira Glass>His columns are reports from the kind of citizen that every country has, the insider outsider. Here are excerpts from those columns translated from Hebrew and read by actor Ramsey Faragallah.
<v Ira Glass>他的专栏是来自每个国家都有的那种公民的报告，内部外部人。这里是由演员拉姆齐·法拉加拉（Ramsey Faragallah）从希伯来语翻译并朗读的那些专栏的摘录。

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:15.430
<v Sayed Kashua>October 18, 2012. It was the coffee machine that ultimately shed new light on my social situation. As usual, it all started because of my wife.
<v Sayed Kashua>2012年10月18日。正是咖啡机最终让我对我的社会状况有了新的认识。和往常一样，这一切都始于我的妻子。

00:46:15.430 --> 00:46:21.350
<v Sayed Kashua>Don't get me wrong. I can be a real jerk, but I do love my wife. Today more than ever.
<v Sayed Kashua>别误解我。我可能是一个真正的混蛋，但我确实爱我的妻子。今天比以往任何时候都更爱。

00:46:21.350 --> 00:46:36.110
<v Sayed Kashua>I've mentioned before that our apartment suddenly started to get on her nerves. At first there were trivial comments like, "These shower hooks are really annoying," or "There's not even a proper place for the shoe closet," even though we never had a shoe closet.
<v Sayed Kashua>我以前提过我们公寓突然开始让她紧张。起初有一些琐碎的评论，比如，“这些淋浴钩真的很烦人，”或“甚至没有鞋柜的合适地方，”即使我们从来没有过鞋柜。

00:46:36.110 --> 00:46:47.160
<v Sayed Kashua>The complaints gathered momentum after our third child was born. "I think we need another room for the baby." "We have to paint. Have you seen the walls? They're already black from the kids' hands."
<v Sayed Kashua>在我们的第三个孩子出生后，抱怨声愈演愈烈。“我想我们需要另一个房间给宝宝。”“我们必须刷墙。你看到墙了吗？它们已经因为孩子的手而变黑了。”

00:46:47.160 --> 00:47:03.520
<v Sayed Kashua>Very quickly, the home that I loved so much, in the building I love, in the neighborhood I love became a nightmare. So I asked my wife, "What do you say to renovating?"
<v Sayed Kashua>很快，我非常喜欢的家，在我喜欢的建筑里，在我喜欢的街区，变成了一场噩梦。所以我问我的妻子，“你觉得翻新怎么样？”

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:33.470
<v Sayed Kashua>I don't know how or why, but of the three renovating teams who were bidding to just basically build a plaster wall and paint the place, I found myself choosing the team that gave me a price three times higher than that of any of the others, an Ashkenazi architect with a Mizrahi interior designer. Maybe it was the iPad that the interior designer used when he presented the work plan. Or maybe it was just because every time he said "plaster wall, " he added the adjective "designer."
<v Sayed Kashua>我不知道怎么或为什么，但在三个投标只是基本上建造一个石膏墙并粉刷那个地方的翻新团队中，我发现自己选择了那个报价比其他任何一个都高三倍的团队，一位带有米兹拉希室内设计师的阿什肯纳兹建筑师。也许是因为室内设计师在展示工作计划时使用的iPad。或者也许仅仅是因为每次他说“石膏墙”时，他都加上了形容词“设计师”。

00:47:33.470 --> 00:47:58.320
<v Sayed Kashua>I closed the deal with the Ashkenazi, who promised to go with my wife to all the stores to choose the color of the designer plaster wall, pick out the shoe closet, and find the hooks that best suited her needs. Because the most important thing in interior design is for the house to meet your needs, said the interior designer, pointing at my wife.
<v Sayed Kashua>我和阿什肯纳兹达成了协议，他承诺去和我的妻子去所有的商店选择设计师石膏墙的颜色，挑选鞋柜，并找到最适合她需求的钩子。因为室内设计中最重要的事情是房子要满足你的需求，室内设计师说，指着我的妻子。

00:47:58.320 --> 00:48:08.450
<v Sayed Kashua>"14,000 shekels for a paint job!" my father shouted at me. "For what? On every electricity pole there is a sign, paint your home for 1,800 shekels."
<v Sayed Kashua>“油漆工作要14,000谢克尔！”我父亲对我大喊。“为了什么？在每一根电线杆上都有一个标志，粉刷你的家要1,800谢克尔。”

00:48:08.450 --> 00:48:14.070
<v Sayed Kashua>"What 1,800 shekels?" I said. "And anyway, we're talking about designer paint, Dad!"
<v Sayed Kashua>“什么1,800谢克尔？”我说。“而且无论如何，我们谈论的是设计师油漆，爸爸！”

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:28.090
<v Sayed Kashua>My father still lives in the village where I grew up, Tira. He tried to explain to me that I'm an imbecile. That in Tira, you could get a new shower and a paint job with a plaster wall, even a designer one, for a tenth of the price.
<v Sayed Kashua>我父亲仍然住在我成长的村庄，提拉（Tira）。他试图向我解释我是个白痴。在提拉，你可以以十分之一的价格得到一个新的淋浴和带石膏墙的油漆工作，甚至是一个设计师的。

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:44.560
<v Sayed Kashua>I tried to explain to him that I don't live in Tira, that the interior designer promised that the finished work would last a lifetime. And that anyway, there was nothing that could be done about it. Life in West Jerusalem in my neighborhood costs a little more, with all due respect to Tira.
<v Sayed Kashua>我试图向他解释我不住在提拉，室内设计师承诺完成的工作会持续一生。而且无论如何，对此无能为力。在我街区的西耶路撒冷生活成本更高，对提拉的所有应有的尊重。

00:48:44.560 --> 00:48:54.720
<v Sayed Kashua>"You're a dunce," my father concluded. "Do what you like."
<v Sayed Kashua>“你是个笨蛋，”我父亲总结道。“做你喜欢的。”

00:48:54.720 --> 00:49:06.320
<v Sayed Kashua>I felt like making coffee. At least I wanted to, but the milk frother in the machine-- which you have to belong to an exclusive customer club in order to use-- had stopped working. I called customer service.
<v Sayed Kashua>我想喝咖啡。至少我想，但机器里的奶泡器——你必须属于一个独家客户俱乐部才能使用它——已经停止工作了。我拨打了客服。

00:49:06.320 --> 00:49:13.720
<v Sayed Kashua>"That's right," I was told after a lengthy hold. "The machine is still under warranty." It would be picked up for repair within three working days.
<v Sayed Kashua>“没错，”在长时间的等待后，我被告知。“机器仍在保修期内。”它将在三个工作日内被取走维修。

00:49:13.720 --> 00:49:35.850
<v Sayed Kashua>In the meantime, while I was waiting for the coffee machine, I realized that the plaster wall and paint job had morphed into a replacement of old doors, installation of an entirely new air conditioning system called something like Inventor, and the sudden urgent need for a new bedroom set whose size would be compatible with the modern design. "Adjustable, " my wife called it.
<v Sayed Kashua>同时，当我等待咖啡机时，我意识到石膏墙和油漆工作已经演变成了更换旧门，安装一个名为Inventor之类东西的全新空调系统，以及对一套现代设计兼容尺寸的新卧室家具的突然紧急需求。“可调节，”我的妻子称之为。

00:49:35.850 --> 00:49:54.830
<v Sayed Kashua>The interior designer also suggested moving the piano to a different location, and building an acoustic wall that would contain the sound. And he proposed that I please put some life into my anemic study. And I agreed, even though I didn't understand exactly what that meant.
<v Sayed Kashua>室内设计师还建议将钢琴移动到不同的位置，并建造一个包含声音的声学墙。他还建议我请为我贫血的研究室注入一些生活。我同意了，尽管我不完全理解那意味着什么。

00:49:54.830 --> 00:50:25.270
<v Sayed Kashua>His iPad has a calculator, and a few quick clicks produced the sum of more than 100,000 shekels, not counting the shoe closet. It took me a moment to recall that I had intended to spend no more than 20,000 shekels. I lapsed into depression, because I had already made the down payment and because I didn't want to tell my wife that we might not be able to meet the future payments. So I said nothing, and she gave me a designer smile such as I hadn't seen for a few years.
<v Sayed Kashua>他的iPad有一个计算器，几次快速点击就产生了超过100,000谢克尔的总和，不计算鞋柜。我花了一会儿才想起我打算花费不超过20,000谢克尔。我陷入了抑郁，因为我已经支付了首付，而且因为我不想告诉我的妻子我们可能无法支付未来的费用。所以我什么也没说，她给了我一个我几年没见过的设计师微笑。

00:50:25.270 --> 00:50:42.810
<v Sayed Kashua>A few days later, on the day when we were supposed to get our prestigious coffee machine back from repairs between 5:00 and 7:00 PM, she said, "Do you  know what's most unsuited to my needs in this apartment?" "Yes," I said. "All the inconvenient things. But everything is going to be fine."
<v Sayed Kashua>几天后，在我们应该从维修中拿回我们久负盛名的咖啡机的那天，在下午5:00到7:00之间，她说，“你知道这间公寓里最不适合我需求的是什么吗？”“是的，”我说。“所有不方便的事情。但一切都会好起来的。”

00:50:42.810 --> 00:50:49.620
<v Sayed Kashua>"No," she said. "I think I'm deceiving myself with all this new design and renovation."
<v Sayed Kashua>“不，”她说。“我觉得我用所有这些新设计和翻新来欺骗自己。”

00:50:49.620 --> 00:50:53.940
<v Sayed Kashua>"I don't understand," I said, genuinely not understanding.
<v Sayed Kashua>“我不明白，”我说，真正不明白。

00:50:53.940 --> 00:51:05.680
<v Sayed Kashua>"What bothers me the most," she said, "is that since we've moved here, you haven't wanted to barbecue. I so much want you to barbecue like we used to back in the village every Friday."
<v Sayed Kashua>“最困扰我的是，”她说，“因为自从我们搬到这里，你就不想烧烤了。我非常希望你像我们以前在村子里每个星期五那样烧烤。”

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:15.290
<v Sayed Kashua>"Barbecue?" I felt my blood pressure rising. "I'm going to spend $100,000 shekels for an interior designer and all you really want is a barbecue?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“烧烤？”我感到我的血压升高。“我要花100,000谢克尔请室内设计师，而你真正想要的只是烧烤？”

00:51:15.290 --> 00:51:25.510
<v Sayed Kashua>"Well," she said, "I miss the village. And the days when you weren't embarrassed to barbecue next to the neighbors." At that point, the phone rang.
<v Sayed Kashua>“嗯，”她说，“我想念村庄。还有那些你不为在邻居旁边烧烤感到尴尬的日子。”就在那时，电话响了。

00:51:25.510 --> 00:51:35.180
<v Sayed Kashua>"Hello," the voice on the phone said as I started to foam at the mouth. "I'm from the delivery company. I have a coffee machine for you. What's the address?" he asked in Arabic.
<v Sayed Kashua>“喂，”当我开始口吐白沫时，电话里的声音说。“我来自快递公司。我为你有一台咖啡机。地址是多少？”他用阿拉伯语问。

00:51:35.180 --> 00:51:45.620
<v Sayed Kashua>I gave him the address. He was silent for a while. And then he said, "But I only deliver in East Jerusalem. I'm sorry. I was told you were an Arab."
<v Sayed Kashua>我给了他地址。他沉默了一会儿。然后他说，“但我只在东耶路撒冷送货。抱歉。有人告诉我你是个阿拉伯人。”

00:51:45.620 --> 00:52:01.320
<v Sayed Kashua>"You know what?" I replied in Arabic, "Forget it. I don't want the espresso machine. I don't even like that kind of coffee."
<v Sayed Kashua>“你知道吗？”我用阿拉伯语回答，“算了。我不要浓缩咖啡机。我甚至不喜欢那种咖啡。”

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:13.310
<v Sayed Kashua>November 22, 2012. The renovations have begun. The apartment is in chaos and I've decided that I'm going to buy the most comfortable bed I can find, and this time I won't even look at the price.
<v Sayed Kashua>2012年11月22日。翻新已经开始了。公寓一片混乱，我决定我要买我能找到的最舒适的床，这次我甚至不会看价格。

00:52:13.310 --> 00:52:23.230
<v Sayed Kashua>I picked up my wife from work and we went to buy the bed of our dreams. "We want the most comfortable bed you have in the store," I declared to the saleswoman who approached us with a smile.
<v Sayed Kashua>我接我妻子下班，我们去买我们梦想的床。“我们要店里你能有的最舒适的床，”我向带着微笑走向我们的女售货员宣布。

00:52:23.230 --> 00:52:29.680
<v Sayed Kashua>"Of course," she said, leading us to the area of the double beds. "Are you interested in an adjustable bed?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“当然，”她说，带领我们到双人床区域。“你对可调节床感兴趣吗？”

00:52:29.680 --> 00:52:37.950
<v Sayed Kashua>"Only adjustable," I declared festively. And the polite saleswoman smiled once again and accompanied us to the area of the adjustables.
<v Sayed Kashua>“只可调节，”我节日般地宣布。礼貌的女售货员再次微笑，陪我们到可调节区域。

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:43.380
<v Sayed Kashua>"Ah," I sighed as I sprawled on the mattress and closed my eyes.
<v Sayed Kashua>“啊，”我躺在床垫上闭上眼睛时叹了口气。

00:52:43.380 --> 00:52:46.090
<v Sayed Kashua>"We also have it in 6 and 1/2 feet wide."
<v Sayed Kashua>“我们也6.5英尺宽。”

00:52:46.090 --> 00:52:56.290
<v Sayed Kashua>"6 and 1/2 feet, hmm." I said to the saleswoman. "Exactly how much does the same bed cost in that size?" When she told me, I shouted. "How much?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“6.5英尺，嗯。”我对女售货员说。“那个尺寸同样的床到底要多少钱？”当她告诉我时，我大喊。“多少？”

00:52:56.290 --> 00:52:59.920
<v Sayed Kashua>"I'll ask the store manager. Maybe he can give you a better price," she said.
<v Sayed Kashua>“我会问店经理。也许他能给你一个更好的价格，”她说。

00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:10.500
<v Sayed Kashua>"That's insane," I said to my wife when the saleswoman went to call the manager. "All that money for what? For foam, a few springs, and the remote control of a toy racing car?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“这太疯狂了，”当女售货员去给经理打电话时，我对我的妻子说。“所有的钱为了什么？为了泡沫，几根弹簧，和玩具赛车的遥控器？”

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:15.410
<v Sayed Kashua>"You said you didn't care about the price," she said. "Why do you even bother making declarations like that?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“你说你不在乎价格，”她说。“为什么你甚至还要费心做出那样的声明？”

00:53:15.410 --> 00:53:28.010
<v Sayed Kashua>"Why? Did I think those were going to be the prices? I was willing to waste up to 5,000 shekels. And I thought that for 4,000 shekels, I would be able to get the best mattress on the market."
<v Sayed Kashua>“为什么？我以为那是会是那个价格？我愿意浪费高达5,000谢克尔。我以为对于4,000谢克尔，我就能买到市场上最好的床垫。”

00:53:28.010 --> 00:53:33.710
<v Sayed Kashua>"Well, apparently this is the real price of mattresses," she said.
<v Sayed Kashua>“嗯，显然这就是床垫的真实价格，”她说。

00:53:33.710 --> 00:53:45.420
<v Sayed Kashua>"Shalom," said the store manager, who came up to us with an amazingly wide smile. He introduced himself and offered his hand, and I introduced myself when I shook his hand.
<v Sayed Kashua>“沙洛姆（Shalom，你好），”店经理说，他带着惊人的宽大微笑向我们走来。他自我介绍并伸出手，当我握手时我介绍了自己。

00:53:45.420 --> 00:53:53.640
<v Sayed Kashua>"Pleased to meet you, 'Sa-eed,'" he continued in Hebrew, mispronouncing my name. I didn't correct him. Neither did my wife.
<v Sayed Kashua>“很高兴见到你，‘Sa-eed’，”他继续用希伯来语说，误读了我的名字。我没有纠正他。我的妻子也没有。

00:53:53.640 --> 00:54:05.050
<v Sayed Kashua>He was quite nice, the manager, despite the prices he was asking. And he had a slight speech problem. He spoke very slowly, emphasizing each letter and nodding his head after every word.
<v Sayed Kashua>他相当不错，经理，尽管他要求的价格。他有一个轻微的言语问题。他说话非常慢，强调每个字母，并在每个词后点头。

00:54:05.050 --> 00:54:16.270
<v Sayed Kashua>(SPEAKING SLOWLY) "You know about this mattress?" he asked, naming the brand that every child in Israel is familiar with.
<v Sayed Kashua>（慢速说话）“你知道这个床垫吗？”他问，列举了以色列每个孩子都熟悉的品牌。

00:54:16.270 --> 00:54:32.450
<v Sayed Kashua>"Yes," I replied in the same vein out of politeness, so he wouldn't think, god forbid, that my fast speech was meant to mock him. (SPEAKING SLOWLY) "We've heard about that mattress."
<v Sayed Kashua>“是的，”我出于礼貌用同样的方式回答，这样他就不会想，上帝保佑，我的快速说话是为了嘲笑他。（慢速说话）“我们听说过那个床垫。”

00:54:32.450 --> 00:54:41.480
<v Sayed Kashua>"So you know which store you've come to?" He smiled and nodded.
<v Sayed Kashua>“所以你知道你来到哪个店了吗？”他微笑着点头。

00:54:41.480 --> 00:54:45.880
<v Sayed Kashua>"Yes," I answered, and nodded too, smiling slowly.
<v Sayed Kashua>“是的，”我回答，也点头，微笑着慢慢地。

00:54:45.880 --> 00:55:02.790
<v Sayed Kashua>I liked him, that manager. I also liked the chain for not hesitating to hire a manager with a speech impediment. I was already thinking of buying the mattress, although I had planned to spend a quarter of that amount. But it would be worth it to support a business whose owners are, first of all, human beings.
<v Sayed Kashua>我喜欢他，那个经理。我也喜欢这个连锁店不犹豫雇佣一个有言语障碍的经理。我已经想买那个床垫了，尽管我计划只花那个金额的四分之一。但支持一个其所有者首先是人类的企业是值得的。

00:55:02.790 --> 00:55:15.910
<v Sayed Kashua>Suddenly he stopped talking to answer the cellphone that was ringing in his pants pockets. "Hello!" he answered with a speed that left me and my wife amazed. "Moshe, I'll get back to you in 15 minutes. [INAUDIBLE]. Bye." And he hung up.
<v Sayed Kashua>突然他停止说话去接他在裤子口袋里响的手机。“喂！”他回答时速度惊人，让我和我的妻子感到惊奇。“莫舍（Moshe），我15分钟后回你。[听不清]。拜。”他挂了。

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:30.660
<v Sayed Kashua>(SPEAKING SLOWLY) "So where were we?" he continued slowly while nodding. I looked at my wife, who looked back at me. "Ah yes," he said. "The mattress."
<v Sayed Kashua>（慢速说话）“所以我们在哪里？”他慢慢继续，同时点头。我看着我的妻子，她看着我。“啊是的，”他说。“床垫。”

00:55:30.660 --> 00:55:35.810
<v Sayed Kashua>"Listen," I said to him at top speed. "Listen, we know what this damn mattress is. Do you understand?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“听着，”我以最高速度对他说。“听着，我们知道这个该死的床垫是什么。你明白吗？”

00:55:35.810 --> 00:55:39.060
<v Sayed Kashua>"Yes," he answered in a panic. "But why are you angry?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“是的，”他在恐慌中回答。“但你为什么生气？”

00:55:39.060 --> 00:55:43.270
<v Sayed Kashua>"Because we may be Arabs," I answered irritably, "but we aren't idiots!"
<v Sayed Kashua>“因为我们可能是阿拉伯人，”我恼怒地回答，“但我们不是白痴！”

00:55:43.270 --> 00:55:50.730
<v Sayed Kashua>"God forbid!" apologized the manager, putting his hand on his chest. "I definitely didn't mean that! I really didn't. I'm sorry if that's how it seemed to you!"
<v Sayed Kashua>“上帝保佑！”经理道歉，把手放在胸前。“我绝对没那个意思！我真的没有。如果你觉得那样，我很抱歉！”

00:55:50.730 --> 00:55:59.290
<v Sayed Kashua>My wife took her bag and decided that we weren't buying anything from someone like him. "Not on your life!" I said as we started making our way out of the store. "Racist idiot!"
<v Sayed Kashua>我的妻子拿上她的包，决定我们不从像他这样的人那里买任何东西。“一辈子都不会！”我说，当我们开始走出店门时。“种族主义白痴！”

00:55:59.290 --> 00:56:01.870
<v Sayed Kashua>"Wait!", he chased after us. "I apologize!"
<v Sayed Kashua>“等一下！”他追我们。“我道歉！”

00:56:01.870 --> 00:56:05.810
<v Sayed Kashua>"Forget it," I answered firmly. "We're not doing business with someone like you!"
<v Sayed Kashua>“算了，”我坚定地回答。“我们不和像你这样的人做生意！”

00:56:05.810 --> 00:56:08.080
<v Sayed Kashua>"I'll give you a big discount," he pleaded.
<v Sayed Kashua>“我给你一个大折扣，”他恳求。

00:56:08.080 --> 00:56:12.820
<v Sayed Kashua>"Forget about it," said my wife. "It's not a matter of discount. It's a matter of principle."
<v Sayed Kashua>“算了吧，”我的妻子说。“这不是折扣的问题。这是原则的问题。”

00:56:12.820 --> 00:56:15.710
<v Sayed Kashua>"Look, I'll take off 25%. What do you say?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“看，我去掉25%。你觉得怎么样？”

00:56:15.710 --> 00:56:21.670
<v Sayed Kashua>"I said no," said my wife, with the determination of a social worker who believes that a principle is a principle.
<v Sayed Kashua>“我说不，”我的妻子说，有着相信原则就是原则的社会工作者的决心。

00:56:21.670 --> 00:56:27.560
<v Sayed Kashua>"I'll give it to you for half price!" insisted the manager, who really was sorry that he had spoken slowly.
<v Sayed Kashua>“我半价给你们！”经理坚持说，他真的很抱歉他说话慢了。

00:56:27.560 --> 00:56:33.890
<v Sayed Kashua>"Principles are principles," continued my wife on her way out of the store, while I stopped in my tracks.
<v Sayed Kashua>“原则就是原则，”我的妻子在走出商店的路上继续说，而我停下了脚步。

00:56:33.890 --> 00:56:36.160
<v Sayed Kashua>"Half price? Are you serious?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“半价？你是认真的吗？”

00:56:36.160 --> 00:56:41.580
<v Sayed Kashua>"I swear," he said. "And I've never done that for anyone, but I really didn't intend to insult you."
<v Sayed Kashua>“我发誓，”他说。“而且我从来没为任何人这样做过，但我真的没打算侮辱你。”

00:56:41.580 --> 00:56:45.780
<v Sayed Kashua>"Sayed!" my wife shouted at me. "Where are your values?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“赛义德！”我的妻子对我大喊。“你的价值观在哪里？”

00:56:45.780 --> 00:57:06.520
<v Sayed Kashua>"Forget values," I told her as I shook the hand of the nice salesman. "The man told you (SPEAKING SLOWLY) half price. Do you understand?" And with tears in his eyes, the salesman said that he would also give us pillows at a big discount.
<v Sayed Kashua>“忘了价值观，”我告诉她，当我和这位不错的推销员握手时。“男人告诉你（慢速说话）半价。你明白吗？”带着他眼里的泪水，推销员说他也会给我们大折扣的枕头。

00:57:06.520 --> 00:57:22.240
<v Sayed Kashua>December 6, 2012. It's been three weeks of renovation and we've started moving back into our apartment, even though it's still not completely finished. When we got there, two Arab workers from a company that specializes in cleaning renovated houses were toiling in the rooms.
<v Sayed Kashua>2012年12月6日。翻新已经三周了，我们已经开始搬回我们的公寓，尽管它仍然没有完全完成。当我们到达那里时，来自一家专门清洁翻新房屋的公司的两名阿拉伯工人在房间里辛勤工作。

00:57:22.240 --> 00:57:36.540
<v Sayed Kashua>I felt uncomfortable at having Arabs cleaning my place, but I just nodded and told the contractor who was supervising the renovation that everything was packed and in the car. And that as soon as he gives the go ahead, I will start taking in the suitcases and bags.
<v Sayed Kashua>让阿拉伯人清洁我的地方让我感到不舒服，但我只是点头，告诉监督翻新的承包商所有东西都已打包并在车里。而且只要他点头，我就会开始搬行李箱和包。

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:46.510
<v Sayed Kashua>Besides the cleaners, the installer of the banister was also supposed to come. "Two hours of work," the contractor said. "He won't interfere with the cleaners and the cleaners won't interfere with him."
<v Sayed Kashua>除了清洁工，栏杆的安装工也应该来。“两个小时的工作，”承包商说。“他不会干扰清洁工，清洁工也不会干扰他。”

00:57:46.510 --> 00:57:54.440
<v Sayed Kashua>The banister guy arrived exactly on time. "This is Sayed," the contractor said as he introduced me. The man nodded and smiled.
<v Sayed Kashua>栏杆的人准时到达。“这是赛义德，”承包商在介绍我时说。那个人点头微笑着。

00:57:54.440 --> 00:58:00.120
<v Sayed Kashua>"Great, Sayed. There might be sparks, so I want you to follow me with a brush and fix up after me."
<v Sayed Kashua>“太棒了，赛义德。可能会有火花，所以我希望你跟着我拿把刷子，事后修复。”

00:58:00.120 --> 00:58:04.310
<v Sayed Kashua>"No," the contractor said, embarrassed. "He's the owner."
<v Sayed Kashua>“不，”承包商尴尬地说。“他是房主。”

00:58:04.310 --> 00:58:11.660
<v Sayed Kashua>"Ah," the installer said, and got to work.
<v Sayed Kashua>“啊，”安装工说，然后开始工作。

00:58:11.660 --> 00:58:23.520
<v Sayed Kashua>"You know," my wife said to me on that sleepless night before the move back, "the house is really charming now. It looks prestigious and comfortable. But still--" she stopped.
<v Sayed Kashua>“你知道，”搬回前那个失眠的夜晚，我的妻子对我说，“房子现在真的很迷人。它看起来久负盛名和舒适。但仍然——”她停住了。

00:58:23.520 --> 00:58:25.720
<v Sayed Kashua>"Still what?" I asked.
<v Sayed Kashua>“仍然什么？”我问。

00:58:25.720 --> 00:58:38.710
<v Sayed Kashua>"I don't know," she said, as she always does when she knows. "I'm just thinking about the children again. About what this is doing to them. How they feel. How it will affect their behavior and the way they get along."
<v Sayed Kashua>“我不知道，”她说，当她知道时她总是这样说。“我只是又在想孩子。关于这对他们有什么影响。他们感觉如何。它将如何影响他们的行为和他们相处的方式。”

00:58:38.710 --> 00:58:41.630
<v Sayed Kashua>"Again the thing with the Jewish neighborhood?" I said.
<v Sayed Kashua>“又是关于犹太街区的事情？”我说。

00:58:41.630 --> 00:58:47.590
<v Sayed Kashua>"I'm not saying anything," she said. "It's a lovely apartment. Very nice neighbors. But you know."
<v Sayed Kashua>“我什么都没说，”她说。“这是一个可爱的公寓。很好的邻居。但你知道。”

00:58:47.590 --> 00:59:03.880
<v Sayed Kashua>"No. I absolutely, absolutely do not know," I replied with the jangled nerves of someone who had planned to spend only 20,000 shekels to add a room with a plaster wall, but found himself paying, as of that moment, more than 180,000 shekels.
<v Sayed Kashua>“不。我绝对，绝对不知道，”我用一个计划只花20,000谢克尔加一个带石膏墙的房间，但发现自己支付，截止那一刻，超过180,000谢克尔的人的神经紧张回答。

00:59:03.880 --> 00:59:14.920
<v Sayed Kashua>"I'm just asking myself," she said, striking a dramatic pose so the words would sink in. "I'm just asking myself if this is our natural place. And even more so for the children."
<v Sayed Kashua>“我只是问我自己，”她说，摆出一个戏剧性的姿势，以便这些词沉进去。“我只是问我自己这是不是我们的自然地方。而且对于孩子来说更是如此。”

00:59:14.920 --> 00:59:18.990
<v Sayed Kashua>"It is natural," I said. "It's 100% natural."
<v Sayed Kashua>“它是自然的，”我说。“它是100%自然的。”

00:59:18.990 --> 00:59:27.720
<v Sayed Kashua>The banister guy came up to me smiling. "I hope you'll excuse me," he said. "I just, I never imagined that you live here. You know what I mean, Sayed."
<v Sayed Kashua>栏杆的人笑着向我走来。“我希望你能原谅我，”他说。“我只是，我从没想过你住在这里。你知道我的意思，赛义德。”

00:59:27.720 --> 00:59:44.660
<v Sayed Kashua>"Sure," I said. "Sure, forget it." And I really meant that, because it didn't hurt me in the least that he thought I was an Arab laborer. At least it didn't hurt me as much as the fact that I had two Arab workers doing their thing in my apartment and I had tried to avoid contact with them until one of them came over and spoke to me in Hebrew.
<v Sayed Kashua>“当然，”我说。“当然，算了。”我真的那样认为，因为他认为我是阿拉伯劳工一点也不伤害我。至少它没有伤害我，因为我有两名阿拉伯工人在我的公寓里做他们的事情，而我一直试图避免与他们接触，直到他们中的一个走过来用希伯来语和我说话。

00:59:44.660 --> 00:59:51.970
<v Sayed Kashua>"These shoes were thrown out," said the worker, who I estimated was my father's age. "They were in the garbage. Can I have them for the kids?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“这些鞋子被扔掉了，”工人说，我估计他是和我父亲一样的年龄。“它们在垃圾里。我可以给孩子吗？”

00:59:51.970 --> 01:00:19.990
<v Sayed Kashua>I choked up for an instant, not sure whether to smile, reply in Hebrew, and play the generous Jewish homeowner who takes pity on the wretches, or to answer in Arabic and come across as a maniac Arab who lives among Jews and throws out almost-new Nikes that were brought from the US in the wrong size for the kids. I found myself saying, [ARABIC] "Please, go ahead," in Arabic. And continuing in Arabic, "Of course you can take them. And there are plenty more we had planned to donate if you want to have a look."
<v Sayed Kashua>我一瞬间噎住了，不确定是该微笑，用希伯来语回复，并扮演那个对可怜人表示同情的慷慨犹太房主，还是用阿拉伯语回答，并以一个生活在犹太人中间，扔掉从美国带来尺寸不对几乎新的耐克的疯狂阿拉伯人出现。我发现自己说，[阿拉伯语] “请，继续，”用阿拉伯语。并继续用阿拉伯语，“当然你可以拿走它们。如果你想看一下，我们还计划捐赠更多。”

01:00:19.990 --> 01:00:24.020
<v Sayed Kashua>"Your Arabic is excellent," the worker said, still speaking in Hebrew.
<v Sayed Kashua>“你的阿拉伯语很好，”工人说，仍然用希伯来语说话。

01:00:24.020 --> 01:00:26.140
<v Sayed Kashua>"I am an Arab," I declared.
<v Sayed Kashua>“我是一个阿拉伯人，”我宣布。

01:00:26.140 --> 01:00:30.590
<v Sayed Kashua>The worker did a double take and asked, "And this is your place?"
<v Sayed Kashua>工人第二次看，并问，“而这是你的地方？”

01:00:30.590 --> 01:00:34.680
<v Sayed Kashua>"Yes," I told him. "We bought it a few years ago. Yes, this is my home."
<v Sayed Kashua>“是的，”我告诉他。“我们在几年前买的。是的，这是我的家。”

01:00:34.680 --> 01:00:40.190
<v Sayed Kashua>"Wella," he said, still amazed. "I never imagined. [FOREIGN LANGUAGE], thank you."
<v Sayed Kashua>“Wella，”他说，仍然惊奇。[外语]，谢谢。”

01:00:40.190 --> 01:00:45.760
<v Sayed Kashua>"For what?" I said. I didn't understand what he was thanking me for and I thought for a moment he was teasing me.
<v Sayed Kashua>“为了什么？”我说。我不明白他在为我感谢什么，我想了一会儿他在取笑我。

01:00:45.760 --> 01:00:50.480
<v Sayed Kashua>"The shoes," he said, holding them up with one hand to show me.
<v Sayed Kashua>“鞋子，”他说，用一只手举起它们给我看。

01:00:50.480 --> 01:01:02.000
<v Sayed Kashua>"You're welcome," I said. My face burned with shame.
<v Sayed Kashua>“不客气，”我说。我的脸因为羞耻而灼热。

01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:14.400
<v Sayed Kashua>I talked to my wife late that night after the children were asleep. "What can I do?" I said leaning back a little in my bed. "And what exactly is our natural place? What?"
<v Sayed Kashua>孩子睡着后，那天深夜我和我的妻子交谈。“我能做什么？”我躺在床上向后靠了一点说。“而什么是我们的自然地方？什么？”

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:20.300
<v Sayed Kashua>"I don't say it's easy," she said, invoking her social work phrases again. "It can be very hard."
<v Sayed Kashua>“我不说它很容易，”她说，再次援引她的社会工作短语。“它可能非常困难。”

01:01:20.300 --> 01:01:28.670
<v Sayed Kashua>"So are you telling me that I have to live in an Arab village to feel I'm in a natural place?" I asked. "I'm not allowed city life?"
<v Sayed Kashua>“所以你在告诉我，我必须住在阿拉伯村庄才能感到我在自然的地方？”我问。“我不被允许城市生活？”

01:01:28.670 --> 01:01:32.710
<v Sayed Kashua>"That's not what I'm saying," she said with a patience that I was quickly losing.
<v Sayed Kashua>“那不是我在说的，”她说，带着一种我很快失去的耐心。

01:01:32.710 --> 01:01:46.200
<v Sayed Kashua>"Then what are you saying?" I insisted. "I really want to understand. What are my options for being natural? Hmm? To buy a crappy apartment in an Arab neighborhood in Jerusalem? To buy a place in the area that was conquered in 1967?
<v Sayed Kashua>“那你是在说什么？”我坚持。“我真的想理解。我自然的选择是什么？嗯？在耶路撒冷的阿拉伯街区买一套破旧的公寓？在1967年被征服的地区买一个地方？

01:01:46.200 --> 01:01:57.050
<v Sayed Kashua>What, what is natural? To go back to the village? Back to Tira to the family? To fights between siblings over every meter of land? What is natural?
<v Sayed Kashua>什么，什么是自然的？回村子？回到提拉去找家人？为每一米土地与兄弟姐妹争吵？什么是自然的？

01:01:57.050 --> 01:02:04.880
<v Sayed Kashua>And what can I do in order to safeguard the children? You tell me that, please! What is right and what is wrong? And I will do it!"
<v Sayed Kashua>而我为了保护孩子能做什么？请你告诉我！什么是对的，什么是错的？而我会做它！”

01:02:04.880 --> 01:02:11.230
<v Sayed Kashua>"I say you should go to sleep now," she said, rubbing cream into her hands and yawning. "You look tired."
<v Sayed Kashua>“我说你应该现在去睡觉，”她说，把霜擦在手上打着哈欠。“你看上去很累。”

01:02:11.230 --> 01:02:28.780
<v Sayed Kashua>"Yes," I said. "Goodnight." And I knew I would not sleep soundly. Or wake up naturally.
<v Sayed Kashua>“是的，”我说。“晚安。”我知道我不会睡得安稳。或自然地醒来。

01:02:28.780 --> 01:02:41.470
<v Ira Glass>Actor Ramsey Faragallah, reading excerpts from newspaper columns first published in Haaretz , written by Sayed Kashua. Kashua's latest novel-- it's been translated into English-- is called Second Person Singular .
<v Ira Glass>演员拉姆齐·法拉加拉，阅读首次发表在《国土报》（Haaretz）上的报纸专栏摘录，由赛义德·卡什亚撰写。卡什亚的最新小说——它已被翻译成英语——被称为《第二人称单数》（Second Person Singular）。

